  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1035 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nTemperatures and wind will be the main focus through Saturday. \nNormal highs this time of year are generally in the upper 30s to\nlower 40s. Forecast highs today are expected to be in the \nmiddle 50s, followed by a three day stretch of highs in the middle \n50s to lower 60s from Thursday through Saturday. Overnight lows \nwill also be mild and close to the climatological maximum for this\ntime of year. Saturday morning holds the best chance to see a few\nrecord high minimum temperatures, provided it does not cool off \ntoo fast before midnight, with lows in the upper 40s to around 50.\nThis same pattern contributing to the mild conditions will also \nallow for windy conditions at times, especially from late morning \nthrough sunset each day, as south to southwest winds gust from \nabout 20 to 30 mph. \n\nSeveral model cycles have kept the Thu-Fri weather system well\nnorth of the forecast area now. Confidence in dry weather through\nFriday has increased. The main impact from this system will be to\nbriefly turn winds to a westerly direction Thursday afternoon \nbefore turning back to the south on Friday.\n\nWeekend precipitation potential remains about the same, although\ntiming has sped up from previous runs. There could be some light\nprecipitation Saturday morning, with the best chances Saturday\nafternoon into Saturday night. The probability for any measurable\nrain has risen slightly to 85 to 95%, while the probability for \nat least 0.50\" of rain has tightened up a bit to 35-50%. \n\nCooler air wrapping around the departing low keeps a small snow\nchance in far north-central Wisconsin Sunday afternoon and \nevening. Probabilities for 0.1\" of snow in far north-central\nWisconsin (mainly Vilas County) nudged up slightly to 20-60%, and\ndrop off pretty fast to the south. Potential for 1\" of snow in \nVilas County is still 10-20%.",
